---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import ProseWithToc from '../../components/ProseWithToc.astro'

const recipesA = `grammar Settings version "1.0.0" edition "v2" {
  pq = *( " " )
  stage = ( 1*( %x41-5A ) ) as stage
  code = ( 1*( %x30-39 ) ) as code
  pair = pq stage pq ":" pq code pq

  bindings pair {
    stage -> stage (string) preprocess: stage_code
    code -> code (integer)
  }

  preprocess stage_code {
    const_map "ISO" -> "iso", "IEEE" -> "ieee"
    default_when_absent "unknown"
  }

  test {
    accept "ISO:42"
    example "IEEE:7" { stage: "ieee", code: 7 }
  }

  entry identifier: pair
}`

const recipesB = `resync document = 1*( %x00-3A / %x3C-FF ) ";"

# the recovering path returns the envelope, not an exception:
{ ok: false,
  error:   { offset: 13, line: 1, column: 14, expected: [...] },
  captures: { k: [{k: "a"@0}], v: [{v: "1"@2}] },
  partial:  ...,
  resyncs:  [{ offset: 4, skipped: "###;", resumed_at: 8 }] }`

const recipesC = `render default {
  field stage
  literal "-"
  field code
}

derive urn "urn:{stage}:{code}"

artifact.render("identifier", bound)   # => "ISO-42"
artifact.derive("urn", bound)          # => "urn:ISO:42"
$ parg generate demo.artifact.json --model model.json
ISO-42`

const recipesD = `$ parg export demo.artifact.json --target xgrammar
$ parg export demo.artifact.json --target llguidance`

const recipesE = `# helper.parg — an import helper (no compiled artifact of its own)
grammar Tokens version "1.0.0" {
  digit = %x30-39
}

# main.parg
use tokens

grammar Main version "1.0.0" {
  number = 1*tokens.digit as num
  entry main: number
}`

const shippingRules = `$ parg fmt grammar.parg -i              # canonical style before commit
$ parg lint grammar.parg --captures    # dead captures, on demand
$ parg compile grammar.parg -o a.json  # bake (a failing test fails the bake)
$ parg diff a.old.json a.json          # classify the change: capture renames
                                       # are major, new alternatives minor,
                                       # literal fixes patch
# the artifact's "validated" receipt is inspectable — check
# lint_errors (always 0), tests_failures (always 0), self_test on
# any artifact you receive`

const sections = [
  { id: 'rules', label: 'Authoring rules' },
  { id: 'patterns', label: 'Decision patterns' },
  { id: 'recipes', label: 'Recipes' },
  { id: 'shipping', label: 'Shipping rules' },
]
---
<Layout
  title="The PARG playbook — rules, patterns, guardrails — Parsanol"
  description="How to use PARG well: the rules the compiler enforces and why, the decision patterns (trivia, atomic, tiers, preprocess, recovery), verified end-to-end recipes, and the shipping discipline."
>
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
  <div class="flex gap-12">
    <aside class="w-64 flex-shrink-0 hidden lg:block">
      <div class="sticky top-20 text-sm space-y-1">
        <a href="/parg" class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-gray-800">What is PARG?</a>
        <a href="/parg/language" class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-gray-800">Language tour</a>
        <a href="/parg/playbook" class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded bg-primary-100 dark:bg-primary-900/30 text-primary-700 dark:text-primary-300 font-medium">Playbook</a>
        <a href="/playground" class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-gray-800">Playground</a>
        {sections.map((section) => (
          <a href={`#` + section.id} data-toc-link class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-gray-800">{section.label}</a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </aside>

    <ProseWithToc rail={false}>
      <h1>The PARG playbook</h1>
      <p class="text-lg">
        The <a href="/parg/language">language tour</a> is the syntax reference. This page is how to
        use it well: the rules the compiler enforces (and why), the decision patterns that come up
        in real grammars, recipes verified end-to-end on the shipped engine, and the discipline for
        shipping a grammar as a dependency.
      </p>

      <h2 id="rules">Authoring rules</h2>
      <p class="mb-4">The compiler enforces these at bake time — they are failures, not warnings:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Order dependence is a build failure.</strong> Alternatives that share leading bytes make ordered choice decisive; the lint names the rule and branches (<code>rule primary: branches 1 and 2 are order-dependent (shared first bytes; ordered choice is decisive)</code> — a real baked warning from the boolean-algebra grammar). Factor the shared prefix or accept and document the order.</li>
        <li><strong>A failing test fails the bake</strong>, and the compiler refuses to emit with lint errors — the artifact's <code>validated.lint_errors</code> is always 0 by construction.</li>
        <li><strong>Keywords cannot name rules</strong> (<code>grammar as alt from_table column bindings preprocess entry table_lookup render derive</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>One rule style per file</strong> — line-terminated or brace bodies; the lints flag heavy mixing.</li>
        <li><strong>Case-insensitive literals capture the raw input</strong>: <code>%i"iso"</code> against <code>IEEE</code> captures <code>"IEEE"</code> — pin expectations with the input's spelling.</li>
        <li><strong>An absent optional capture's key is omitted, not nil</strong> — and <code>default_when_absent</code> fills nil, not omission. Pin expectations against the actual tree (the tour's tests section shows how).</li>
        <li><strong>Binding cardinality must agree with the grammar's shape</strong> — a repetition bound <code>1..*</code> must be a repetition; the compile fails naming the mismatch.</li>
        <li><strong>Capture every structural rule</strong> so each repetition iteration yields one hash — the parslet-compatible flattening idiom.</li>
        <li><strong><code>##</code> doc comments ride the artifact</strong> as LSP hovers — document every entry-reachable rule.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="patterns">Decision patterns</h2>
      <div class="card overflow-x-auto mb-4">
        <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-700 text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white whitespace-nowrap">when you're tempted to…</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-white">do this instead</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-700">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">hand-roll whitespace handling between tokens</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Declare <code>skip = trivia</code> once — or explicit <code>pq</code> rules when positions matter. Token spellings stay contiguous either way; multi-character operators never match across whitespace.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">Mark everything atomic "for speed"</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200"><code>atomic</code> is for token-shaped bodies (identifiers, numbers) where trivia injection would be wrong — it trades skip injection for shape control, not performance.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">Reach for runtime state or custom Ruby transformers</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">First check the declarative vocabulary: <code>table_lookup</code>, <code>const_map</code>, <code>default_when_absent</code>, <code>rename</code> cover data normalization. State atoms make the grammar <strong>Ruby-tier by contract</strong> — the Rust, wasm, TS, and Python engines reject the artifact loudly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">Normalize data in the model layer by hand</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">If it's a pure data transform (map, fill, rename), it belongs in <code>preprocess</code> — the engines execute one implementation, byte-identically. Anything not expressible there is genuinely application logic.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">Trial-scan for resynchronization</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Declare a <code>resync</code> policy: deterministic junk runs ending at a terminator, authored and baked as spans.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">Copy-paste token rules between grammars</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200"><code>use</code> a helper grammar — rules arrive namespaced (<code>tokens.digit</code>), capture names preserved, the expansion baked.</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">Reparse to render output strings</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200"><code>render</code>/<code>derive</code> sections in the artifact — the string side ships with the grammar, byte-identical on every tier.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="recipes">Recipes — verified end-to-end</h2>

      <h3>The full pipeline</h3>
      <pre class="text-sm"><code>{recipesA}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Grammar, bindings with a v1.1 preprocess, in-file tests pinning <code>example</code> bounds —
        one file, one bake. The compiler runs the tests; a failing expectation fails the build.
      </p>

      <h3>Corrupt input, owned recovery</h3>
      <pre class="text-sm"><code>{recipesB}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The strict path throws <code>ParseFailed</code> at the deepest position. The recovering path
        returns the envelope: what failed and where, everything that bound before the failure, the
        partial tree, and each span the declared <code>resync</code> policy consumed — offsets
        included. The author owns the recovery; the engine owns determinism.
      </p>

      <h3>The round trip</h3>
      <pre class="text-sm"><code>{recipesC}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Parse → bind → render back: fields read the bound map, templates interpolate it, and the
        CLI does it from the artifact alone — a bound attribute map in, the default-variant string out.
      </p>

      <h3>Ship to the decoders</h3>
      <pre class="text-sm"><code>{recipesD}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The same artifact lowers to XGrammar or llguidance for LLM structured output — unmappable
        constructs rejected loudly at export, never silently dropped.
      </p>

      <h3>Compose, don't copy</h3>
      <pre class="text-sm"><code>{recipesE}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Helper grammars share tokens across files; imports are recursive, cycles are compile errors,
        and the artifact bakes the expansion so consumers never resolve imports.
      </p>

      <h2 id="shipping">Shipping rules</h2>
      <pre class="text-sm"><code>{shippingRules}</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Grammar CI compares behavior, not bytes</strong>: the envelope's <code>semantics_hash</code> is stable across re-bakes by a newer compiler — a consumer gate can warn on producer drift and fail only on a real grammar change.</li>
        <li><strong>Versioning is classified</strong>: capture renames are major, new alternatives minor, literal fixes patch — <code>parg diff</code> names the class against the documented rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Trust, but inspect</strong>: the <code>validated</code> receipt travels the artifact — lint errors, test totals, and the self-test flag are covered by both digests, so "was this baked green" is a field read, not a promise.</li>
        <li><strong>Distribute through the artifact, not the source</strong>: the .parg text rides inside; consumers rebuild from the envelope without the file.</li>
      </ul>
    </ProseWithToc>
  </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
